  message: "SATISFIED — dev 25a608a625. Closed on the item's OWN filed repro, re-measured\non the merged tree.\n\nTHE FIX. `_census_languages` is one home for \"which languages does the taint\narm count\", production-scoped by the same imported classifier the flow filter\nand the edge scoping already use, and `--include-non-production-sources`\nwidens it in step with them. `languages` (unscoped) stays the census for the\nBOUNDARY arm and the catalog loop, which answer a different question — what\nmust be loaded to classify every edge, fixtures included. Boundary verdicts\nuntouched.\n\nDIRECTION DECIDED, NOT GUESSED. This item was filed with its fork stated\nbecause \"does a fixture count?\" is a threat-model question. WI-kozos's scope\nstatement (merged ae67059b51) answers it: the audit covers the INSTALLED CLI.\n\nMY FIRST VERSION DID NOT WORK, and the item's own repro is what caught it.\nScoping by \"does this node's path classify as a test\" left the five languages\nthis item names STILL in the census: on the real self-survey go, java, rust,\nswift and elixir survived on 9, 9, 8, 4 and 1 `<external>` nodes, with zero\nproduction source files between them. An `<external>` node is a reference\nTARGET, minted because some other file named it — counting it as evidence the\nlanguage is present in shipped code inverts its meaning. The unit tests were\ngreen throughout. Only the real-data A/B showed it.\n\nPresence now requires a real source file. `paths.names_no_source_file` is the\nshared predicate, promoted from the private copy `sketch._map_source_paths`\nhas carried since INV-jumim — the identical question, already asked once, in a\nsecond home (L53). sketch migrated to it in the same PR.\n\nONE OF MY OWN TESTS HAD ENCODED THE WRONG RULE (a path-less node counts as\nproduction). Corrected in place, carrying the measurement that refutes it,\nrather than deleted.\n\nMEASURED, bash peeled so this layer is the front blocker, using production's\nown supported-language filter (43,488 nodes / 152,565 edges):\n  before  \"supported language(s) elixir, go, java, rust, swift were analyzed\n           but produced no call edges, so their I/O is invisible\"\n  after   GONE. Front blocker becomes layer 3: \"the analysis launches an\n          external program at 81 call site(s)\"\nCensus 24 languages -> 11. That is the four-layer stack draining as filed, and\nlayer 3's answer already shipped as the opaque_boundary caveat kind (rc 3).\n\nNO USER-VISIBLE CHANGE TODAY and the item does not claim one: bash is layer 1\nand still fires first, so the live self-proof stays 18/18 inconclusive at rc 2\n(\"no taint catalogue (bash)\"), verified on the shipped CLI post-merge.\nINV-vavup is the owner-decided route to that layer.\n\nTHE CONTROL THAT KEEPS THIS HONEST is pinned in the suite:\ntest_a_PRODUCTION_language_that_emits_nothing_STILL_blocks. The simpler\nnarrowing — keep only languages that produced scoped call edges — passes the\nrepro and destroys the check, because it also drops a language that IS\nproduction and emits nothing (Kotlin, ~95% of catalogued sinks unreachable).\nThat would turn the F69.A1 blindness signal into a silent pass, in the\nconfirm-ward direction.\n\nFILED WHILE MEASURING: INV-hosul (P3) — with layer 3 exposed, the caveat\nrenders each bash site as `./scripts/auto-pr../scripts/auto-pr`. Display only;\nthe site set is correct."  # 8fa4
